WEBVTT

00:00:00.395 --> 00:00:02.920
Listener: Why is the refund for order one
oh four stuck?

00:00:03.415 --> 00:00:04.965
Guide: The payment call timed out.

00:00:04.965 --> 00:00:07.365
That trace tells us what the refund job
saw.

00:00:07.365 --> 00:00:10.790
Each dot here is a node, one source record
in this picture.

00:00:10.790 --> 00:00:15.015
In Kubernetes, the owner link tells us
which deployment owns a ReplicaSet.

00:00:15.015 --> 00:00:17.665
A network flow names the two ends of a
connection.

00:00:17.665 --> 00:00:19.040
We call a link a typed edge

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:21.640
because its type tells us what the
connection means.

00:00:22.870 --> 00:00:25.220
Listener: What ties this reading to that
storage account?

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:27.990
Guide: They share a full Azure resource
ID.

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:30.340
Azure Resource Graph names the storage
account,

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:33.090
and Azure Monitor puts that same ID on its
reading.

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:34.515
That’s an identity match.

00:00:34.515 --> 00:00:36.665
Sattle’s ontology supplies the naming rule

00:00:36.665 --> 00:00:38.940
that ties both records to one account.

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:40.490
Now follow the DNS name.

00:00:40.490 --> 00:00:44.115
It resolves to an address, and the private
endpoint names the account.

00:00:44.115 --> 00:00:46.115
These links connect different things.

00:00:47.328 --> 00:00:49.603
Listener: What happens to that path when
we group the dots?

00:00:50.148 --> 00:00:51.548
Guide: It stays connected.

00:00:51.548 --> 00:00:53.373
Follow the DNS name to the address,

00:00:53.373 --> 00:00:55.873
the private endpoint and the storage
account.

00:00:55.873 --> 00:00:57.848
The dots move into technical groups,

00:00:57.848 --> 00:01:00.073
but the links still cross those
boundaries.

00:01:00.073 --> 00:01:03.148
A projection is a rule for building one
graph from another.

00:01:03.148 --> 00:01:06.748
This one groups records by technology so
you can see how DNS,

00:01:06.748 --> 00:01:09.223
networking and Azure resources fit
together.

00:01:10.453 --> 00:01:12.328
Listener: Where does that leave the refund
job?

00:01:12.873 --> 00:01:14.298
Guide: It belongs to Payout.

00:01:14.298 --> 00:01:17.673
A capability groups software by the job it
does for the shop.

00:01:17.673 --> 00:01:20.248
The job reads a case from Returns Intake.

00:01:20.248 --> 00:01:25.423
Its call to the payment API becomes the
blue arrow from Payout to Payment API.

00:01:25.423 --> 00:01:27.248
That arrow between two groups stands for a

00:01:27.248 --> 00:01:29.673
call you can trace back to the refund job.

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:33.420
Listener: Which view would help me
investigate the timeout?

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:35.315
Guide: Start with the dashboard.

00:01:35.315 --> 00:01:37.040
It counts successful replies.

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:39.940
Two out of two payment calls succeeded in
the first minute.

00:01:39.940 --> 00:01:42.265
By the last minute, neither got a reply.

00:01:42.745 --> 00:01:45.145
The health tree shows what that means for
Payout.

00:01:45.145 --> 00:01:47.545
Its rule needs a successful payment call.

00:01:47.545 --> 00:01:50.420
Payout is blocked, and the order lookup
still works.

00:01:50.900 --> 00:01:53.525
The Kubernetes view answers a different
question.

00:01:53.525 --> 00:01:55.450
Which pods run the refund job?

00:01:55.450 --> 00:01:58.000
Follow the owner links down from the
deployment.

00:01:58.455 --> 00:02:00.455
The Azure resource view lists the storage

00:02:00.455 --> 00:02:02.505
account in the shop’s resource group.

00:02:02.985 --> 00:02:07.310
The DNS and IP view shows how its name
reaches a private address.

00:02:07.310 --> 00:02:09.985
Each view selects what it needs from the
same records.

00:02:09.985 --> 00:02:13.135
That’s why you can follow one problem
across all five.

00:02:14.770 --> 00:02:18.420
Listener: If the database stops answering,
why does web turn red too?

00:02:18.990 --> 00:02:22.465
Guide: Web needs the API, and the API
needs the database.

00:02:22.465 --> 00:02:23.765
Look at the two arrows.

00:02:23.765 --> 00:02:26.940
The blue one points from the database
service to the server it needs.

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:28.415
The red one points back.

00:02:28.415 --> 00:02:30.165
A failure can affect the caller.

00:02:30.165 --> 00:02:32.915
That’s health roll-up, and it explains why
the database,

00:02:32.915 --> 00:02:36.140
API and web all read Unhealthy in this
revision.

00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:39.720
Listener: What makes the database red in
the first place?

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:42.340
Guide: One bad minute opens the alarm.

00:02:42.340 --> 00:02:45.440
The chart shows the meter dropping to zero
at ten eleven.

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:48.015
Azure Monitor delivers that reading a
minute later.

00:02:48.015 --> 00:02:49.465
Zero is a measurement.

00:02:49.465 --> 00:02:51.865
The database rule gives it meaning for
health.

00:02:51.865 --> 00:02:55.465
On this rule, one failed sample makes the
database Unhealthy.

00:02:56.687 --> 00:02:59.487
Listener: Why does the alarm reach web and
leave the cache green?

00:03:00.057 --> 00:03:03.157
Guide: Web has a path to the failing
database through the API.

00:03:03.157 --> 00:03:04.957
The cache has no such path.

00:03:04.957 --> 00:03:07.882
Follow the arrows to see what each service
depends on.

00:03:07.882 --> 00:03:10.082
The structure query builds those links.

00:03:10.082 --> 00:03:12.757
The health query follows up to sixteen
Affects links

00:03:12.757 --> 00:03:14.857
and takes the worst state it can reach.

00:03:14.857 --> 00:03:16.832
That’s why the cache stays green.

00:03:18.037 --> 00:03:19.387
Listener: The meter is back at one.

00:03:19.387 --> 00:03:21.312
Why is the database still red?

00:03:21.832 --> 00:03:24.757
Guide: The rule remembers how many good
minutes came in a row.

00:03:24.757 --> 00:03:28.182
At ten fourteen the count is one, and the
alarm stays open.

00:03:28.182 --> 00:03:29.582
Ten fifteen makes two.

00:03:29.582 --> 00:03:31.957
The alarm clears and the callers recover.

00:03:31.957 --> 00:03:34.357
The times across the top come from the
meter.

00:03:34.357 --> 00:03:38.232
Revision eighteen names the version of the
graph that records the recovery.

00:03:39.878 --> 00:03:43.228
Listener: Suppose an API pod starts
sending traffic to Redis.

00:03:43.228 --> 00:03:44.853
What does Sattle see first?

00:03:45.398 --> 00:03:49.048
Guide: Hubble recorded one flow from the
API pod to this address.

00:03:49.048 --> 00:03:52.598
In our copy of the replay, the pod and the
address already exist.

00:03:52.598 --> 00:03:55.023
The new line means the pod sent traffic
there.

00:03:55.023 --> 00:03:56.698
That’s what the network observed.

00:03:56.698 --> 00:03:59.673
To call Redis a dependency of the whole
API service,

00:03:59.673 --> 00:04:01.948
the model needs to know who’s at each end.

00:04:03.187 --> 00:04:06.762
Listener: How does that address become a
Redis dependency in the health model?

00:04:07.282 --> 00:04:10.407
Guide: Kubernetes ties the pod to the API
service.

00:04:10.407 --> 00:04:14.057
At the other end, the address matches a
private endpoint for Redis.

00:04:14.057 --> 00:04:16.382
The DNS name points to the same cache.

00:04:16.382 --> 00:04:19.932
That gives the structure query both ends
of a service dependency.

00:04:19.932 --> 00:04:23.082
It derives the API to Redis link from
those records.

00:04:23.082 --> 00:04:26.382
The health model can now follow it using
its existing rule.

00:04:27.603 --> 00:04:28.978
Listener: Redis was already there.

00:04:28.978 --> 00:04:30.553
Where can I find the new link?

00:04:31.098 --> 00:04:32.448
Guide: Look for the plus sign.

00:04:32.448 --> 00:04:35.573
A patch lists the edits from one graph
revision to the next.

00:04:35.573 --> 00:04:38.923
In the master graph, the added line means
the pod sent traffic.

00:04:38.923 --> 00:04:42.498
In the structure graph, it means the API
depends on Redis.

00:04:42.498 --> 00:04:45.798
In the health graph, revision fourteen
adds that dependency.

00:04:45.798 --> 00:04:47.573
The cache was there all along.

00:04:48.812 --> 00:04:52.412
Listener: How could I check that the API
really started using that cache?

00:04:52.957 --> 00:04:55.157
Guide: Follow the dependency back through
the graphs.

00:04:55.157 --> 00:04:57.282
The structure query kept two matches.

00:04:57.282 --> 00:05:00.207
One links the pod’s destination to a
private endpoint.

00:05:00.207 --> 00:05:02.882
The other identifies Redis by its DNS
name.

00:05:02.882 --> 00:05:06.482
That trail from a conclusion back to its
inputs is called provenance.

00:05:06.482 --> 00:05:08.032
Now compare the two runs.

00:05:08.032 --> 00:05:10.232
Redis has the same load warning in both.

00:05:10.232 --> 00:05:14.282
The added dependency explains why the API
and gateway turn amber.
